00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:17.360
bound as a statesman to consult, he was swift, zealous, radical, and determined. Taking him

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:24.750
all in all, measuring the tremendous magnitude of the work before him, considering the necessary

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:29.960
means to ends, infinite wisdom has seldom seen.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:33.100
sent any man into the world better fitted for his mission

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:38.780
than Abraham Lincoln, Frederick Douglass.

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:53.910
On April 26th, the Union cavalry trapped John Wilkes Booth

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:56.570
in a Virginia tobacco barn and set it afire.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:59.720
His accomplice, David Harold, surrendered.

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:01.470
Booth preferred death.

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:03.540
A soldier shot him in the neck.

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:07.910
At the end, he asked to have his hands raised,

00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:10.100
looked at them, and said,

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:12.260
useless, useless.

00:28:16.180 --> 00:28:19.780
That day, in a farmhouse near Durham Station, North Carolina,

00:28:20.660 --> 00:28:22.440
Confederate General Joseph Johnston

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:24.420
surrendered what was left of his army

00:28:24.420 --> 00:28:26.130
to William Tecumseh Sherman.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:37.080
Jefferson Davis, exhausted but still defiant, fled southward, hoping somehow to rally the Confederacy from Texas.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:44.130
It may be that with a devoted band of cavalry, I can force my way across the Mississippi,

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:52.520
and if nothing can be done there, then I can go to Mexico and have the world from which to choose a location.

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:59.160
On May 10th, at Irwinville, Georgia, Union cavalry caught up with him.

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:04.810
With the arrest of its president, the Confederate government ceased to exist.

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:08.690
Davis was sent north to Virginia under heavy guard.

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:15.790
Northern newspapers spread the false rumor that Davis had been apprehended wearing women's clothes.

00:29:16.550 --> 00:29:20.260
North and south, he was reviled as the villain of the war.

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:27.540
These misconceptions about Davis are so strange that it's as if a gigantic conspiracy was launched.

00:29:28.090 --> 00:29:29.890
It was partly launched by Southerners.

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:33.230
who, having lost the war, did not want to blame it on their generals,

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:35.500
so they blamed it on the politicians.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:37.680
And, of course, Davis was the chief politician.

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:42.120
So it was the Southerners more than the Northerners who vilified Jefferson Davis.

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:46.310
The Northerners wanted to hang him from a sour apple tree,

00:29:46.330 --> 00:29:49.530
but the Southerners really tore him down after the war.

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:55.310
Davis was imprisoned at Fortress Monroe in a cell kept perpetually lit

00:29:55.330 --> 00:29:57.520
and was made to wear chains,

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.970
though he protested that those are...

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:09.050
orders for a slave, and no man with a soul in him would obey such orders. Dear Verena, this is not

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:17.080
the fate to which I invited you when the future was rose-colored for us both, but I know you will

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:25.770
bear it even better than myself, and that of us two. I alone will ever look back reproachfully on

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:26.250
my career.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:37.710
Scattered fighting stuttered on in Louisiana, Alabama, and Mississippi, and even further west,

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:46.080
where on May 13, 1865, Private John J. Williams of the 34th Indiana became the last man killed

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:53.920
in the Civil War in a battle at Palmito Ranch, Texas. The final skirmish was a Confederate victory.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:09.640
On the morning of May 23rd, 1865, the American flag flew at full staff above the White House for the first time since Lincoln's death.

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:21.100
U.S. Grant and the new president, Andrew Johnson, stood side by side to watch the grand armies of the Republic pass in review down Pennsylvania Avenue from the Capitol.

00:31:24.950 --> 00:31:29.910
And so it came, this glorious old army of the Potomac, for six hours.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:34.160
marching past, 18 or 20 miles long,

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:36.420
their colors telling their sad history.

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:41.750
It was a strange feeling to be so intensely happy and triumphant

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:43.970
and yet to feel like crying.

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:52.680
The great procession took two days.

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:58.840
General George Armstrong Custer stole the show the first day.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:02.620
developing past the dignitaries far ahead of his men,

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:06.990
brandishing his saber, his long yellow hair, whipping in the wind.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:10.920
The crowds cheered loudest the next morning.

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:16.860
William Tecumseh Sherman rode past the head of the great army he had led to the sea.

00:32:23.290 --> 00:32:26.930
By May, most of the Yankees had withdrawn from Clarksville, Tennessee.

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:29.980
What remained of the 49th and 14th...

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:37.570
Tennessee regiments came home. Private John J. Denny of Company K was not among them. He had

00:32:37.570 --> 00:32:45.960
died at Chancellorsville. Of the 29 Stewart College seniors who went to war, 16 had been killed in

00:32:45.960 --> 00:32:57.720
battle. Seven more had died of wounds and disease. In September, railway service to Clarksville was

00:32:57.720 --> 00:32:58.200
resumed.

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:05.600
Deer Isle, Maine was an indirect casualty of the war.

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:09.440
When its men came home, they found fishing had fallen off.

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:13.550
There was new money to be made in other industries and nearby towns.

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:16.630
The old families moved away.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:21.610
Some of the houses they left behind became summer homes for vacationers,

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:25.210
most of whom were unaware of what had happened there.

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:36.290
John Wilkes Booth's accomplices were swiftly tried before a military commission.

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:38.360
All eight were found guilty.

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:43.040
Four were sentenced to be hanged, including Mary Surratt,

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:47.800
whose only crime may have been that she owned the boarding house in which the conspirators met.

00:33:51.110 --> 00:33:55.790
The executions took place in the courtyard of the old penitentiary building on July 7th.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:08.680
climbed the 13 steps and sat in chairs while the charges were read aloud. Two priests comforted

00:34:08.680 --> 00:34:23.190
Mrs. Surratt and shielded her from the sun. White hoods were slipped over their heads. General

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:29.650
Winfield Scott Hancock, the hero of Gettysburg, clapped his hands three times. Soldiers knocked

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:29.970
the front.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:52.160
part of the platform out from under the condemned. It took them more than five minutes to die.

00:34:54.940 --> 00:34:58.940
A northern newspaper said, we want to know their names no more.

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:14.460
Somewhere they crawl to die, alone, in bushes, low gullies, or on the sides of hills.

00:35:15.530 --> 00:35:21.630
There, in secluded spots, their skeletons, bleached bones, tufts of hair, buttons,

00:35:21.830 --> 00:35:25.170
fragments of clothing, are occasionally found yet.

00:35:25.170 --> 00:35:29.970
Our young men, once so handsome,

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:33.200
and so joyous, taken from us,

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:35.170
the son from the mother,

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:37.810
the husband from the wife,

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:41.740
the dear friend from the dear friend,

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:43.860
Walt Whitman.

00:36:01.400 --> 00:36:04.580
Three and a half million men went to war.

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:12.960
620,000 men died in it, as many as in all the rest of America's wars combined.

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:21.800
One quarter of the South's white men of military age were dead.

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:28.490
In Iowa, half the men eligible to fight served in the Union Army,

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:29.980
filling 46...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:47.220
regiments in all. 13,001 Iowans died. 3,540 in battle. 515 while prisoners of war. 8,498

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:55.790
of disease. Those figures were typical. The 5th New Hampshire Regiment started out from

00:36:55.790 --> 00:36:58.590
Concord in 1861 with 1,200 men.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:10.330
returned to New Hampshire after Gettysburg, there were only 380 left. In Mississippi in 1866,

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:20.080
one-fifth of the state's entire budget was spent on artificial limbs. A million left with vivid

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:29.880
memories of men who should have still been living, but were not. The survivors went home and got on.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:38.530
the business of living the morning after my arrival home i doffed my uniform of first lieutenant

00:37:38.530 --> 00:37:43.990
put on some of my father's old clothes and proceeded to wage war on the standing corn

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:51.300
feeling i had was sort of queer it almost seemed sometimes as if i had been away

00:37:51.640 --> 00:37:59.970
only a day or two had just taken up the farm work where i had left off leander stood

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:19.120
Well, formerly 61st Illinois. The boys who had gone off to war were old men now. They walked over the old battlefields with their families, pointing out the places where they had once done things that now seemed impossible, even to them.

00:38:22.260 --> 00:38:29.400
They had a theoretical notion of having a country, but when the war was over,

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:35.080
both sides they knew they'd had a country they'd been there they had walked its hills and tramped

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:42.750
its roads uh they saw the country and they knew they had a country and they knew the effort that

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:49.450
they had expended and their dead friends had expended to preserve it it did that it made

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:51.070
their country in actuality

00:39:07.610 --> 00:39:14.630
By the turn of the century, monuments and memorials and statues stood in city parks and courthouse squares from Maine to Mississippi.

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:21.410
Number 220, statue of American soldier, price $450.

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:29.980
When used as a family monument and photos of the deceased soldier can be furnished, we will model a new head in a true likeness.

00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:32.800
The extra cost will be but $150.

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:36.320
The Monumental Bronze Company, Bridgeport, Connecticut.

00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:45.930
Halls Hill, Virginia, July 4, 1865.

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:50.220
Another Independence Day in the Army, and this has been my fifth.

00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:56.240
The first we passed at Camp Clark near Washington, the second at Harrison's Landon,

00:39:57.350 --> 00:39:59.960
the third at Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, the fourth...

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:07.450
at Petersburg. And today, we are back in Washington with our work finished. The day has been fun.

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:18.970
Elisha Hunt Rhodes. The war made Elisha Hunt Rhodes. Having risen from private to colonel

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:24.130
during the war, he was promoted to brigadier general after it, then went into the cotton

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:29.980
and wool business in Providence. He devoted nearly every idle hour to...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.220
Veterans Affairs and never missed a regimental reunion.

00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:10.080
America has no north, no south, no east, no west.

00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:14.290
The sun rises over the hills and sets over the mountains.

00:41:14.290 --> 00:41:16.330
The compass just points up and down.

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:21.930
And we can laugh now at the absurd notion of there being a north and a south.

00:41:22.770 --> 00:41:24.770
We are one and undivided.

00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:26.040
Sam Watkins.

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:29.980
Sam Watkins.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.260
returned to Columbia, Tennessee, ran the family farm,

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:37.060
and in the evenings worked on his memoirs, Company H.

00:41:37.650 --> 00:41:40.540
Despite, he said, a house full of young rebels

00:41:40.540 --> 00:41:43.360
clustering around my knees and bumping my elbows.

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:52.480
But for the war, these men were like any other possible friends.

00:41:53.860 --> 00:41:58.200
You can remember Thomas Hardy's poem,

00:41:58.400 --> 00:41:59.440
had he and I but met.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:04.160
Some old ancient inn, you might sit down to wet, write many a nipper can, you know, but

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:06.620
range is infantry, standing face to face.

00:42:07.830 --> 00:42:10.150
I shot at him as he had me and killed him in his place.

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:16.060
Strange and curious war is, you shoot a fellow down, you treat where any bar is, or help to

00:42:16.060 --> 00:42:16.760
half a crown.

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:19.270
Isn't that it?

00:42:19.330 --> 00:42:26.040
Especially in our own society, where these men shared a common history, men and women,

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:28.860
shared a common love of liberty.

00:42:30.510 --> 00:42:35.860
gave it slightly different English as it spun through their lives.

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:44.270
But at the same time, when death came and there was no more to fight about,

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:50.930
the sort of ocean of love and respect closed over them again, and they were together.

00:42:52.990 --> 00:42:59.970
I think we understand what military fame is, to be killed on the field of battle and have...

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:09.190
have our names spelled wrong in the newspapers. William Tecumseh Sherman. William Tecumseh

00:43:09.190 --> 00:43:14.950
Sherman remained a soldier, fighting Indians and shunning politics until his retirement in 1883.

00:43:16.080 --> 00:43:21.260
If nominated, I will not run, he told a Republican delegation urging him to run for president.

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:29.680
If elected, I will not serve. He died in New York City in the winter of 1891. Among the honorary

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:36.770
pallbearers who stood bareheaded in the cold wind outside the church was 82-year-old Joe Johnston

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:43.290
would fought Sherman in Georgia and the Carolinas. When a friend warned him he might fall ill,

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:48.420
Johnston told him, if I were in Sherman's place and he were standing here in mine,

00:43:49.060 --> 00:43:54.980
he would not put on his hat. Johnston died 10 days later of pneumonia.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:06.140
1666. There are nights here with the moonlight cold and ghastly and the whippoorwills,

00:44:07.460 --> 00:44:14.640
and screech owls alone disturbing the silence, when I could tear my hair and cry alone for all

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:27.970
that is past and gone. Mary Chestnut. When James and Mary Chestnut returned to Mulberry Plantation,

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:29.880
they found the old house stripped.

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:37.010
by union men. The cotton burned. Mary managed to make a little money selling butter and eggs

00:44:37.010 --> 00:44:43.400
in partnership with her former slave, and she continued to write. But she never completed

00:44:43.400 --> 00:44:56.530
the mammoth task of reworking her war diary. Jefferson Davis was never tried for treason,

00:44:56.890 --> 00:44:59.490
nor could he ever bring himself to ask for a pardon.

00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:07.840
After two years in prison, he was released on bond and spent the rest of his life living off the charity of a wealthy widow,

00:45:08.610 --> 00:45:13.060
working on a massive memoir, The Rise and Fall of the Confederate Government.

00:45:15.290 --> 00:45:19.610
He died still persuaded of the justice of his cause at the age of 81.

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:27.760
Hiram Revels of Mississippi became the first black man ever elected to the United States Senate,

00:45:28.240 --> 00:45:29.960
filling the seat last held by judge.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:30.620
Jefferson Davis.

00:45:33.120 --> 00:45:38.840
Vice President Alexander Stevens was imprisoned briefly and then re-elected to his old congressional

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:42.460
seat from Georgia, as if there had never been a confederacy.

00:45:47.380 --> 00:45:50.600
Mary Todd Lincoln never recovered from her husband's murder.

00:45:51.060 --> 00:45:53.400
Her son Tad died in 1871.

00:45:54.190 --> 00:45:58.990
Five years later, her eldest son Robert had her committed to a mental institution.

00:46:00.220 --> 00:46:06.170
She spent her last years in Springfield, rarely leaving a room whose curtains were never raised.

00:46:10.230 --> 00:46:14.230
For Clara Barton, the angel of the battlefield, the grim work continued.

00:46:15.340 --> 00:46:19.560
After the war, she went down to Andersonville and helped arrange dignified burial

00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:22.340
for thousands of the Union prisoners who had died there,

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:25.140
then went on to found the American Red Cross.

00:46:29.280 --> 00:46:29.960
On November...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:37.100
10, 1865, Henry Wirtz, commandant at Andersonville Prison, was hanged in the yard of the old Capitol

00:46:37.100 --> 00:46:48.120
prison in Washington for war crimes. He pleaded he had only followed orders. Walt Whitman published

00:46:48.120 --> 00:46:56.010
Drum Taps, a book of Civil War poems he thought his finest, then turned largely to prose. His

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:58.350
writings revolutionized American literature.

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:05.020
Phil Sheridan went out west to take on a new enemy,

00:47:05.260 --> 00:47:07.760
declaring that the only good Indian was a dead Indian.

00:47:09.200 --> 00:47:11.360
George Armstrong Custer went west too,

00:47:11.950 --> 00:47:14.630
carrying with him his belief in his own invincibility.

00:47:15.620 --> 00:47:19.570
In 1876, the Sioux and Cheyenne proved him wrong.

00:47:21.190 --> 00:47:23.570
George McClellan stayed abroad for three years

00:47:23.570 --> 00:47:25.190
after losing the election to Lincoln.

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:28.310
He heard no slander about himself there, he said.

00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:29.960
Then he came home.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:32.660
and got himself elected governor of New Jersey.

00:47:34.140 --> 00:47:38.360
The conqueror of Fort Sumter, Pierre Gustave Toutain Beauregard,

00:47:38.520 --> 00:47:42.980
promoted railroads, managed the Louisiana State Lottery, and got rich.

00:47:44.660 --> 00:47:48.200
Nathan Bedford Forrest promoted railroads too, but failed.

00:47:49.960 --> 00:47:54.660
In 1867, he became the first imperial wizard of the Ku Klux Klan,

00:47:55.500 --> 00:47:59.090
but quit. The Klan grew too violent even for him.

00:48:00.980 --> 00:48:05.620
General Dan Sickles somehow escaped court-martial for his blunder at Gettysburg.

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:10.700
He had the leg he lost in the peach orchard mounted in a miniature casket

00:48:10.700 --> 00:48:13.680
and gave it to the Army Medical Museum in Washington,

00:48:13.700 --> 00:48:16.770
where he visited it regularly for 50 years.

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:24.150
John Bell Hood, who had survived some of the fiercest fighting of the war,

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:29.070
died with his wife and daughter in the New Orleans Yellow Fever epidemic of 1878.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.440
leaving 10 orphaned children.

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:36.220
George Pickett never overcame his bitterness

00:48:36.220 --> 00:48:38.840
over the destruction of his division at Gettysburg.

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:40.980
Suffering from severe depression,

00:48:41.260 --> 00:48:44.360
he turned down offers of command from the ruler of Egypt

00:48:44.360 --> 00:48:46.540
and the president of the United States

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:49.540
and ended up in the insurance business.

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:53.340
Confederate General James Longstreet

00:48:53.340 --> 00:48:54.860
joined the Republican Party,

00:48:55.450 --> 00:48:57.190
served as Grant's minister to Turkey,

00:48:57.740 --> 00:48:59.980
dared to criticize Lee's strategy at Gettysburg.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:06.440
Gettysburg and for all these things was considered a traitor to the south by his former comrades in

00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:15.030
arms. Frederick Douglass continued to fight as hard for civil rights as he had against slavery

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:22.530
and became the most powerful black politician in America. A young visitor once asked him what

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:28.330
he should do with his life. Agitate, the old man answered. Agitate, agitate.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:37.400
Julia Ward Howe helped lead the American Woman Suffrage Association for 55 years.

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:45.770
At her funeral in 1910, 4,000 mourners joined in singing Battle Hymn of the Republic.

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:52.950
Colonel Washington Roebling left the Army Corps of Engineers, finished his father's bridge at

00:49:52.950 --> 00:49:57.930
Cincinnati and went on to build the greatest suspension bridge in the world in Brooklyn.

00:50:00.410 --> 00:50:09.340
I have fought against the people of the North because I believe they were seeking to wrest from the South its dearest rights.

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:18.770
But I have never cherished toward them bitter or vindictive feelings, and I have never seen the day when I did not pray for them.

00:50:21.680 --> 00:50:29.970
Robert E. Lee swore renewed allegiance to the United States, and by so doing persuaded thousands of his former soldiers to do the same.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:39.300
same. He was weary, ailing, and without work in the summer of 1865 when an insurance firm offered

00:50:39.300 --> 00:50:47.780
him $50,000 just for the use of his name. He turned it down. I cannot consent to receive pay

00:50:47.780 --> 00:50:55.040
for services I do not render. He ended up in the noble way you might have expected after you'd

00:50:55.040 --> 00:50:59.980
learned to expect it. He didn't know what to do with himself after the war.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:06.480
war, his profession was gone, even his country was gone. And he was approached with a good deal of

00:51:06.480 --> 00:51:12.800
hesitation by these people from a little school called Washington College. And he accepted the

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:20.110
presidency of Washington College at an annual salary of $1,500 and a house to live in. And he

00:51:20.110 --> 00:51:27.110
spent the rest of his life at what after his death was called Washington Lee. Greatest mistake of my

00:51:27.110 --> 00:51:29.830
life, he said, was taking a military education.

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:38.400
And whenever his students and those of the neighboring Virginia Military Institute marched together, Lee made a point of staying out of step.

00:51:41.840 --> 00:51:48.890
He never returned to Arlington again. Once, on his way to Washington, he glimpsed his old home from a passing train.

00:51:51.930 --> 00:51:59.970
He died in 1870. In last moments, he went back to the war, ordering A.P. Hill to bring up his train.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:04.460
just as Stonewall Jackson had on his deathbed at Chancellorsville.

00:52:10.570 --> 00:52:13.340
Then Lee called out, strike the tent.

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:29.270
For he will smile and give you with unflinching courtesy prayer.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:39.090
trapping letters, uniforms and orders, photographs, kindness, valor and advice and do it with such

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:44.650
grace and gentleness that you will know you have the whole of him penned down, mapped out,

00:52:44.980 --> 00:52:53.300
easy to understand. And so you have all things except the heart. The heart he kept a secret

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:56.560
to the end from all the picklocks of biographers.

00:53:02.780 --> 00:53:10.600
I feel that we are on the eve of a new era when there is to be a great harmony between the Federal and the Confederate.

00:53:11.620 --> 00:53:19.730
I cannot stay to be a living witness to the correctness of this prophecy, but I feel it within me that it is to be so.

00:53:22.750 --> 00:53:29.980
The qualities that served Ulysses S. Grant so well in war, stubbornness, independence, aversion to...

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:36.790
politics, deserted him in peacetime. He entered the White House, pledged to peace, honesty,

00:53:36.930 --> 00:53:44.720
and civil rights, but corruption tainted his two terms. After the presidency, he settled

00:53:44.720 --> 00:53:50.480
in Manhattan, where he lent his name to a Wall Street brokerage firm. Another partner

00:53:50.480 --> 00:53:57.900
in the firm stole millions from the shareholders in 1884 and bankrupted the Grant family. Once

00:53:57.900 --> 00:53:59.970
again, U.S. Grant was penalized.

00:54:01.620 --> 00:54:08.250
At almost the same moment, he was found to be suffering from inoperable cancer of the throat.

00:54:10.460 --> 00:54:15.500
Determined to provide for his family before he died, he set to work writing his memoirs.

00:54:16.040 --> 00:54:21.260
In the summer of 1885, he moved to a cottage at Mount McGregor in the Adirondacks.

00:54:23.560 --> 00:54:29.880
Unable now to eat or speak, he sat on the front porch in the afternoons, laboring over his manuscript.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:36.790
He finished it on July 16th and died one week later.

00:54:40.650 --> 00:54:46.190
Grant's memoirs sold half a million copies and restored his family's fortune.

00:55:00.030 --> 00:55:05.110
In 1913, the government held a 50th anniversary reunion at Gettysburg.

00:55:06.070 --> 00:55:07.330
It lasted three days.

00:55:08.980 --> 00:55:14.750
Thousands of survivors bivouacked on the old battlefield, swapping stories, looking up old comrades.

00:55:22.150 --> 00:55:25.050
Climax was to be a reenactment of Pickett's charge.

00:55:25.050 --> 00:55:29.970
As the rebel yell rang out and the old Confederates started forwarding...

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:38.130
again across the fields, a moan, a gigantic gasp of unbelief rose from the Union men on Cemetery Ridge.

00:55:38.800 --> 00:55:44.320
It was then, one onlooker said, that the Yankees, unable to restrain themselves longer,

00:55:44.650 --> 00:55:49.330
burst from behind the stone wall and flung themselves upon their former enemies,

00:55:50.190 --> 00:55:55.990
not in mortal combat, but embracing them in brotherly love and affection.

00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:09.560
pageant has passed the day is over but we linger loath to think we shall see them no more together

00:56:09.560 --> 00:56:20.650
these men these horses these colors of field joshua lawrence chamberlain joshua lawrence

00:56:20.650 --> 00:56:27.660
chamberlain was at the gettysburg reunion still imposing at 83 despite almost constant pain

00:56:27.660 --> 00:56:29.900
from the unhealed internal damage

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:32.780
done him by a Confederate miniball at Petersburg.

00:56:34.020 --> 00:56:37.820
The reunion was, he said, a transcendental experience,

00:56:38.350 --> 00:56:40.650
a radiant fellowship of the fallen.

00:56:42.510 --> 00:56:45.890
He had received the Medal of Honor for his courage at Little Round Top,

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:48.840
served four terms as governor of Maine,

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:51.940
then became president of Bowdoin College,

00:56:52.180 --> 00:56:56.740
where he managed to teach every subject in the curriculum except mathematics.

00:56:58.770 --> 00:56:59.970
He died of his ancient...

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:24.020
wound in 1914. The war was over. Who won the war? The Union army obviously won the

00:57:24.020 --> 00:57:28.220
war in the sense that they were the army left standing and holding their weapons

00:57:28.220 --> 00:57:29.340
when it was all over.

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:41.090
So the soldiers who fought in the Union Army, the generals who directed it, the president who led the country during it, won the war.

00:57:43.330 --> 00:57:49.620
If we're not talking just about the series of battles that finished up with the surrender at Appomattox,

00:57:49.760 --> 00:57:59.960
but talking instead about the struggle to make something higher and better out of the country, then the question gets more complicated.

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:08.960
The slaves won the war and they lost the war because they won freedom, that is, the removal of slavery.

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:13.010
But they did not win freedom as they understood freedom.

00:58:15.450 --> 00:58:29.980
I suppose that slavery is merely the horrible statutory expression of a deeper rift between people based on...

00:58:30.760 --> 00:58:43.570
race and that is what we struggle still to to heal and i think the the significance of lincoln's

00:58:43.570 --> 00:58:51.760
life and his victory was that that we will never again enshrine these concepts into law

00:58:52.260 --> 00:58:57.870
But now let's see what we can do to erase them from the hearts and minds of people.

00:59:00.320 --> 00:59:07.630
The Civil War is not only the central event of American history, but it's a central event in large ways for the world itself.

00:59:08.240 --> 00:59:13.290
If we believe today in the 20th century, as surely we must, that popular government is the way to go,

00:59:13.490 --> 00:59:15.930
it is the way for the emancipation of the human spirit,

00:59:16.700 --> 00:59:20.980
then the Civil War established the fact that a popular government could survive,

00:59:21.300 --> 00:59:25.140
that it could overcome an internal secession movement that could destroy it.

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:29.970
So the war becomes, in essence, it becomes a testament for the liberation.

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:42.110
of the human spirit for all time four million Americans had been freed after

00:59:42.110 --> 00:59:46.920
four years of agony but the meaning of freedom in American life remained

00:59:46.920 --> 00:59:53.760
unresolved the emancipated slaves own nothing one Tennessee planter wrote

00:59:54.170 --> 00:59:59.970
because nothing but freedom has been given them thousands of black

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:04.580
Blacks wandered southern roads searching for relatives or looking for work or food.

01:00:06.080 --> 01:00:10.900
Thousands more stayed on their plantations as hired hands or sharecroppers.

01:00:12.640 --> 01:00:19.410
The 13th Amendment was followed by a 14th and a 15th, promising full citizenship, due process

01:00:19.410 --> 01:00:21.830
for all American men, white and black.

01:00:23.180 --> 01:00:25.060
But the promises were soon overlooked.

01:00:25.490 --> 01:00:29.900
In the scramble for a new prosperity, white supremacy was brutal.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:37.200
reimposed throughout the old Confederacy. The white South won that war of attrition.

01:00:38.060 --> 01:00:43.770
It would take another century before blacks gained back the ground for which so many had given their

01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:59.640
lives. I think what we need to remember most of all is that civil war is not over until we

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:05.920
today have done our part in fighting it, as well as understanding what happened

01:01:05.920 --> 01:01:15.740
when the Civil War generation fought it. William Faulkner said once that history

01:01:15.740 --> 01:01:24.790
is not was, it's is. And what we need to remember about the Civil War is that the

01:01:24.940 --> 01:01:28.620
Civil War is in the present as well as in the past.

01:01:30.250 --> 01:01:35.330
The generation that fought the war, the generation that argued over the definition of the war,

01:01:35.530 --> 01:01:43.300
the generation that had to pay the price in blood, that had to pay the price in blasted hopes and a lost future,

01:01:44.660 --> 01:01:51.930
also established a standard that will not mean anything until we have finished the work.

01:01:52.760 --> 01:01:57.210
You can say there's no such thing as slavery anymore. We're all citizens.

01:01:58.520 --> 01:01:59.960
But if we're all citizens...

01:02:00.060 --> 01:02:04.380
then we have a task to do to make sure that that, too, is not a joke.

01:02:04.640 --> 01:02:09.690
If some citizens live in houses and others live on the street,

01:02:10.110 --> 01:02:11.810
the civil war is still going on.

01:02:12.190 --> 01:02:16.170
It's still to be fought, and regrettably, it can still be lost.

01:02:38.860 --> 01:02:45.470
gettysburg's guns are still and the dead sleep on america's most famous battleground is a camp

01:02:45.470 --> 01:02:52.100
again with a road dividing the blue and gray there is no other dividing line now as 2,500

